\\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140263/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140263"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-44057"] :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-1\\cdot k)+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-2-2k+18 \\\\  & =16-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 16-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-97347/selection/12b7d840a997b0af25896f37c76d366155c72a970c1525c62ec119b69786f393" :answer/id #uuid "0e9eb6ac-fad9-5bd1-bf8d-ffbceb4bb169" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97347/selection/b5672e3d5d727bcb709f33eeb0f8df47c72e1fe5fb135d6d9ea4ceefea5f0aa1" :answer/id #uuid "824cedf0-acf8-53e3-8e9a-06cbf01e9c4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97347/selection/ac46d580a95a8fc03d1a7cb07de70e107b73fba6bf1bc1d13103b84eff3df012" :answer/id #uuid "8259007d-4ac8-5f97-95af-dc2a81a0510c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97347/selectio